The two code examples represent simple yet functional web-based games—The Guessing Game and Rock, Paper, Scissors (RPS)—both adhering to a fundamental, single-file development approach using HTML for structure, CSS for styling, and JavaScript for game logic. The code's primary strength is its simplicity and portability, allowing either game to be executed directly in any modern browser without external dependencies. The JavaScript sections manage the core game state (like the secret number or player scores), handle user input via button clicks (onclick attributes), and implement the comparison logic necessary to determine outcomes (Too High/Low, Win/Loss/Draw). However, this monolithic structure, where all three technologies are nested in one .html file, creates several code quality issues, including the heavy use of global functions and variables, which compromises encapsulation and increases the risk of naming collisions in a larger project. Furthermore, the RPS game suffers from insecure state management where scores are easily modifiable via the browser console, and both examples lack modern accessibility features and better practices like separating the UI elements from the game logic for improved maintainability.
